+
+### Accepted-source rebuild ownership
+
+The opt-in KVMem source rebuild enters through `Engine::prepare_source_rebuild`, serialized with Engine units. Program reads its own SourceJournalStore, mints a fresh identity with the existing window allocator and prepares an independently charged immutable source copy. Frontend constructs ordinary linear raw-token rows without rendering a chat template again. Program and SourceJournalStore remain exclusive to the immutable model instance; cross-Program/store receipts fail incarnation validation. This is not a tokenizer/model migration mechanism.
+
+The prepared prompt owns preparation until admission. The normal ResourceManager/Program target planner reserves real main/draft/state resources. A recovery request always starts at an empty root and cannot quote or restore an old historical/native prefix. Before prefill the source is branched with separate S accounting, preserving the original source. RequestControl owns the preparation until the first successful whole accepted commit, revalidating on each prefill and before completion. Retention rejects unfinished rebuilds, including cancelled partial prefill; private intermediate checkpoints do not enter retained history. All GPU execution remains on the ordinary Program path.
+
+`historical_sources()` enumerates the existing bounded source directory and derives retained-domain/retired-page observations from current windows, without a second catalog. These are observations, never checkpoint permissions. Recovery/source metadata is process-local; persisted checkpoints, archive aliases and system-wide ForgetSource need their own fences. Full source staging and branch copies have real costs; recovery quota is not an RSS or model-workspace upper bound.
+
+Program publishes the current source receipt after prefill and accepted-output units. Engine copies that receipt into the owning RequestRecord and terminal GenerationResult; it never derives it from the global last-source gauge. This also preserves a partial cancelled input's receipt. The bounded directory reports explicit branch parent/prefix metadata, so clients can distinguish source branches from later independent requests without a second catalog.
